<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Zamua</h1><p class="st-meta">900 BCE – 800 BCE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Assyrian name for the hill lands around the Shahrizor plain, whose chiefs resisted Ashurnasirpal II before becoming an Assyrian province.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Zamua, or Mazamua, covered the plain of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shahrizor">Shahrizor</a> and the valleys around modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulaymaniyah">Sulaymaniyah</a>, the old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantustan">homeland</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lullubi">Lullubi</a>. In the 9th century BCE it was divided among local rulers who controlled the passes between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a> lowlands and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iranian-plateau">Iranian plateau</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Zamua is known almost entirely from Assyrian royal annals. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashurnasirpal-ii">Ashurnasirpal II</a> campaigned there in the 880s BCE against a coalition led by Nur-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadad">Adad</a>, a chief of Dagara, and later against Ameka and Arashtua, destroying fortified towns and imposing tribute of horses, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and wine. Its lands were valued for livestock and as a route east.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>By the late 9th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> had turned Mazamua into a province, governed from Assyrian forts. It remained part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire</a> until the empire collapsed in 612 to 609 BCE.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="tribal confederation, 900 BCE – 800 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry="24"/><path d="M44 34 V78"/><path d="M68 20 V84"/><path d="M68 20 L63 30 H73 Z"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">tribal confederation</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Mesopotamia and Iran</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:41.79%;width:1.99%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>900 BCE – 800 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century-bce">10th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/9th-century-bce">9th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Nur-Adad, Ameka</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Mazamua, Land of Zamua</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-850&amp;focus=zamua" aria-label="Zamua on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5804 960 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Zamua lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6092 1134h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6124 1216h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M6124 1216h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Iraq. <a href="/atlas?year=-850&amp;focus=zamua">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

